            <ArticleTitle>Complement 3 serum levels as a pro-inflammatory biomarker for insulin resistance in obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S229-S232</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1871-4021(16)30228-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.dsx.2016.12.036</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Obesity is frequently characterized by chronic inflammation and insulin resistance (IR). Obesity-associated inflammation is responsible for the complement system activation of which the third component (C3) plays the central role.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to discuss several aspects of the central component of the complement system in relation to obesity and obesity-associated IR.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A critical review of the relevant published English articles from 2003- 2014 was carried out using several search engines including PubMed, Google Scholar, and ScienceDirect. Keywords were used, including complement system, C3, obesity-induced inflammation, IR, ASP, and adipose tissue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The defect in the adipose tissue secretory function during obesity may result in different metabolic disorders including IR. The C3 role during obesity-associated inflammation in IR is emerging. More longitudinal studies are warranted to evaluate the role of C3 among other pro-inflammatory biomarkers for IR.</AbstractText>
